; expect 60
; MAC-CAP-10.5 - what an entry file can and cannot capture from the
; format lowering. Measured rather than asserted; the file exists
; because this repository got the answer wrong in a comment twice.
;
; The lowering invents two free identifiers that no template spells:
; the rendering call for a hole, and the `strConcat` that joins a
; literal run to it. Both were capturable when this file was written.
; NEITHER IS NOW, and they closed separately, which is why both are
; measured here:
;
;  1  THE RENDERING IS NOT CAPTURABLE, since 0.7.4. The expander emits
;     `format#` (`expFmtShow`, self_host/expand.ax), and `#` is not an
;     identifier character - `AX1001` - so no declaration can name the
;     head, and `expFmtShow` does not consult `expQualify` at all.
;     `capture` below answers 2, `fmtInt` on 42, THROUGH a file that
;     declares its own `show`. It answered 3 until 0.7.4, because
;     `xyz` is 3, and that was this fixture's whole subject.
;     ABLATION, run rather than reasoned: put
;     `(expFmtCall env "show" v vsp)` back in `expFmtHole`, rebuild
;     from the seed, and this file exits 40 - `capture` back to 3 and
;     `captureCat` to 5 (`n=` plus `xyz`), with bits 2 and 4 still
;     passing so the failure names itself.
;
;  2  THE ENTRY FILE'S `show` IS STILL A LIVE FUNCTION, and the point
;     is that the lowering does not reach it rather than that it does
;     not exist. `direct` calls it by name and gets its 3 characters.
;     Without this bit, bit 1 would also pass if `show` had simply
;     stopped compiling.
;
;  3  `strConcat` IS NOT CAPTURABLE EITHER, and it closed EARLIER and
;     for a different reason - which is worth a bit of its own,
;     because `self_host/expand.ax` went on claiming the opposite
;     until 0.7.4 and this fixture never asked. `expQualify`'s fourth
;     rule (MAC-HYG-8 hole 4) rewrites a template's free identifier to
;     `Mod$name` when exactly ONE module in the merged declaration
;     list declares it, and `Str` is the only one that declares
;     `strConcat`, so the lowering emits `Str$strConcat` and the entry
;     file's own declaration is not in the running. Measured on the
;     0.7.3 binary as well as this tree: `#calls=` on `main` reads
;     `Str$strConcat` in both.
;     The hole needs a NON-EMPTY literal run to emit a join at all -
;     `expFmtCat` answers the accumulated node itself when there is
;     nothing to join to, so a bare `{n}` emits no `strConcat`, which
;     is also why bit 1's probe is undisturbed by the hijack below.
;     `captureCat` answers 4 - `n=42` - and 8 would be `HIJACKED`.
;
;  4  A SPECIFIER still composes with the rendering rather than
;     replacing it, under both hijacks. This was bit 2 until 0.7.4,
;     where it measured the padding wrapping a CAPTURED rendering;
;     what survives the capture is the composition.
(import IO)


(import Str)

; The two hijacks. Each signature is deliberately type-COMPATIBLE with
; the use the expansion makes of it - an ill-typed capture is loud and
; would prove nothing about the silent one this file was written to
; catch.
(:: show (-> a String))

(fn (show x) "xyz")

(:: strConcat (-> String String String))

(fn (strConcat a b) "HIJACKED")

(:: bit (-> Bool Int Int))

(fn (bit b w)
  (if b
    w
    0
  )
)

; 1: the hole does not go through this file's `show`. 2 is `fmtInt` on
;    42; 3 would be `xyz`.
(:: capture (-> Int Int))

(fn (capture n) (strLen (format "{n}")))

; 2: and `show` is nonetheless a function this file can call.
(:: direct (-> Int Int))

(fn (direct n) (strLen (show n)))

; 3: the literal run's join does not go through this file's
;    `strConcat` either. 4 is `n=42`; 8 would be `HIJACKED`.
(:: captureCat (-> Int Int))

(fn (captureCat n) (strLen (format "n={n}")))

; 4: a SPECIFIER is undisturbed by either hijack - the padding is
;    `Fmt`'s and it wraps the rendering rather than replacing it.
;    6 is `    42`. This was bit 2 of this file until 0.7.4, where it
;    measured the capture feeding the padding; the composition is what
;    survives the capture's removal.
(:: captureWidth (-> Int Int))

(fn (captureWidth n) (strLen (format "{n:>6}")))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let ((n 42))
    (+ (bit (== (capture n) 2) 4) (+ (bit (== (direct n) 3) 8) (+ (bit (== (captureCat n) 4) 16) (bit (== (captureWidth n) 6) 32))))
  )
)
